        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
NASA is planning for long-duration missions beyond low Earth orbit. To sustain crew health and performance and prevent micro-nutritional deficiencies, adequate nutrition must be provided through the food system. The current food system is processed and shelf stable to meet space flight requirements, resulting in insufficiencies of several nutrients. One approach to address this is to grow fresh foods in situ, focusing on dietary variety and nutrients that are deficient in packaged food, or that diminish over time with storage. Dwarf tree fruits have never before been studied for use in space flight, but fresh fruits are rich sources of vitamins and bioactive compounds and could enhance crew diet by expanding nutritional diversity, and by augmenting flavors, aromas, and overall dietary acceptability. Kennedy Space Center (KSC) solely possesses dwarf cultivars of plums (Prunus domestica) that are expected to be compatible with the volume constrained plant growth hardware on the International Space Station (ISS), such as Veggie. Previous studies have shown that plums have the potential to reduce loss of bone density and to increase the intake of potassium and vitamin K, which are both deficient in the packaged food system. This experiment aimed to study the growth and productivity of the dwarf plums in controlled environments under the same environmental conditions found on the ISS (light, temperature, relative humidity and CO2).
<p>
This study had the following specific aims:
<ol>
<li>Establish dwarf plum trees in controlled environment growth chambers and monitor a range of growth and horticultural parameters using flight constraints (growing volumes, environmental conditions, watering techniques, CO2 levels, and electrical lighting).
<li>Establish feasibility of growth protocols and cultivar selection for space flight, and make recommendations to guide future research solicitations.</ol>
<p>
APPROACH: <br>
Plum trees that were genetically modified by USDA researchers to overexpress the FT flowering gene were studied as a candidate crop for space food systems. The FT transformation eliminated dormancy requirements and allowed the plants to flower and develop fruit at a very early age (<1 yr), essentially on dwarf plants. Prior horticultural testing at KSC compared the performance of several GM plum genotypes in controlled environment chambers, and four of the better performing lines, NASA-5, -6, -10, and -11, were used for most of the testing reported here. Tests showed that regular irrigations with Peters Excel 13 N – 2 P - 13 K soluble fertilizer produced good growth using commercial potting mixes. But many plants appeared sensitive to having soils too wet or too dry (based on visual assessments). Tests were conducted to improve cloning techniques using branch cuttings dipped in rooting hormone powder (Indole-3-butyric acid —IBA). Studies were also conducted to monitor leaf transpiration and stomatal conductance rates of several genotypes grown at two CO2 concentrations, since CO2 is known to affect stomatal opening and transpiration. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Fruit development from these GM plants required cross pollination using wild type pollen to get reliable fruit set. Time from pollination to first sighting of fruit was approximately 7 days, with time from pollination to mature fruit showing purple color taking about 68 days. Genotypes NASA-5 and NASA-11 were the most reliable for pollination and fruit set. The fruit flesh at maturity was light green, smooth, and juicy. Fruits saved from these and prior tests were analyzed for elemental composition, carotenoids (lutein and zeaxanthin), ORAC antioxidant values, total phenolics, and Vitamin K. Lutein averaged 0.1-0.2 mg / g dry weight, while zeaxanthin was below detection; Vitamin K ranged from 55-75 µg / 100 g dry weight; ORAC from 122 to 257 TE / g dry weight; and total phenolics from 15-23 mg / g dry weight. Plums and prunes are known to slow or reverse bone loss, which could be linked to their high phenolic content and high ORAC values (Smith et al., 2014). 
<p>
Based on some horticultural challenges and need for cross pollination, the investigators recommend that the ptFT plums undergo further testing before consideration for space flight. Among the challenges will be testing the potential for launching vegetative cuttings, refining branch pruning techniques to maintain short stature, further breeding to get self-compatible pollination, and further understanding plant water and nutritional requirements, particularly for environments like the ISS, which have CO2 levels of ~2500 ppm or higher. But the effects of the FT genetic transformation of plums are dramatic and should be explored with other fruit trees to bring them into the discussions for future space food crops. 
<p>
No datasets exist for this experiment. A final report was archived.
<p>
